# The earnings-calendar sweep. A ONE-SHOT unit, driven by the timer beside it.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT A THREAD IN THE DASHBOARD. Twice now this repo has put work
# that talks to a slow external thing inside the web process and paid for it:
# `/api/overview` re-parsed a 21 MB journal on a 2 s poll until the handler took
# 39 s, and `_fill_tape` seeded itself in the request path and rate-limited
# Alpaca to 429 on the same key the trading worker spends from. A sweep makes
# ~43 HTTP calls to api.nasdaq.com and takes about a minute. It belongs
# out-of-band, writing a cache that the dashboard only ever READS.
#
# WHY A TIMER AND NOT A LOOP. The data changes on a daily cadence at most --
# companies confirm earnings dates days or weeks ahead -- so a long-lived
# process would spend all day asleep. A one-shot unit that exits is also the
# only shape where a failure is unambiguous in the journal.
#
# WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT DO. It writes `state/mktfeed/*.json` and nothing
# else. It places no orders, reads no account, touches no ledger, and needs no
# Alpaca credentials at all -- api.nasdaq.com and data.sec.gov take no key. If
# it never runs, `mktfeed.read_feed` reports a stale horizon and `optcal` goes
# back to answering UNKNOWN, which is the blocking answer. A missing sweep makes
# the system more cautious, never less.
#
# Install (on the VM, as root):
#   cp deploy/tickaverager-mktfeed.service /etc/systemd/system/
#   cp deploy/tickaverager-mktfeed.timer   /etc/systemd/system/
#   systemctl daemon-reload
#   systemctl enable --now tickaverager-mktfeed.timer
#   systemctl start tickaverager-mktfeed          # run it once, now
#   systemctl list-timers tickaverager-mktfeed
#   journalctl -u tickaverager-mktfeed -n 60

[Unit]
Description=Tick Avenger -- earnings calendar sweep (Nasdaq + SEC, no key)
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=coleashcraft_trading
WorkingDirectory=/home/coleashcraft_trading/tickaverager
Environment=PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1

# --days 60 is the default and is stated anyway, because the number is load
# bearing: `mktfeed.MIN_FORWARD_HORIZON_DAYS` refuses to answer off a feed whose
# forward horizon has shrunk below 35 days, so a 60-day sweep run daily always
# has room and a 40-day one would start failing after a week of missed runs.
ExecStart=/home/coleashcraft_trading/tickaverager/venv/bin/python mktfeed.py sweep --days 60

# A sweep that cannot finish in ten minutes is a host that is not answering, and
# the right outcome then is to fail and leave YESTERDAY'S feed in place -- which
# is still honest, because freshness here is judged by the forward horizon and
# not by the file's age. cache_write is atomic for the same reason: a sweep
# killed mid-write must not leave a half-map that parses as a complete one.
TimeoutStartSec=600

StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
SyslogIdentifier=tickaverager-mktfeed
